  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/.... \nThe subtropical high will continue to remain entrenched across the \nregion through at least the end of the week, with 500 mb height \nfields remaining steady between 595-597dam. Therefore, temperatures \nwill continue to run a good 4-7 degrees above normal as afternoon \nhighs across the lower deserts will range between 108-113 degrees. \nThere will also be little overnight relief as an increase in low-\nlevel moisture will cause overnight lows to only bottom out \nbetween the mid 80s to low 90s with the low 90s expected in the \nPhoenix area. Therefore, widespread Major HeatRisk will continue \nto remain in place with Extreme Heat Warnings in effect through \nFriday evening. \n\nThe latest guidance does show the high eventually moving \nnortheastward into the Four Corners region by the end of the week \ninto next weekend, putting the region under a more favorable \nmoisture advection pattern as the east to southeast flow \nincreases. Therefore, an increase in monsoonal activity is more \nlikely during this timeframe, mostly across the higher terrain \nareas. Although, it will certainly be possible for convective \nactivity to impact the south-central AZ lower deserts at some \npoint as well, it is still too far out to know what days could be \nactive as it will be dependent on mesoscale processes such as \noutflow evolutions and/or any easterly waves that are difficult to\npinpoint several days out. As of right now, NBM PoPs across the \nsouth-central AZ lower deserts starting next Thursday range \nbetween 10-20%.",
